|Corners were rounded and plates were revalidated for 1943 with white tabs, for 1944 with green tabs, and for 1945 with white tabs, due to metal conservation for [[World War II]]. Front plates were removed and replaced with windshield stickers around 1943. Large plates stopped issuing at {{nowrap|720 000}} in 1942 and resumed issuing in 1943.<ref name="Tanner" />
|-
|[[File:Wisconsin 1942 Wartime passenger license plate.jpg|150x150px]] [[File:Blank License Plate Shape.svg|150px]] [[File:Blank License Plate Shape.svg|150px]] [[File:Blank License Plate Shape.svg|150px]]
|Late 1942-late 1943
|Embossed golden yellow serial on black plate with border line; "WIS" at bottom left, embossed oval with debossed date in bottom right corner
|''none''
|A12, A1B, AB1, ABC, 12A, 1A2, 1AB, A123, 1A23
|A00 to ZZZ, 00A to 9ZZ, A000 to Z999, 0A00 to 9K99<ref name="Tanner" />
|Plates were small to save metal for the war. Plates used every serial possible in a three digit format then overflowed to a four digit format; serial formats for three digit plates would progress from A00 to A09, then A0A and so on, treating the letters like numbers. I, L, M, O, Q, and W were not used in serials.<ref name="Tanner" /> Early plates were dated 1942 with the oval the same color as the serial, rest of plates were dated 43 and the oval was white. Plates were validated to 1945 with horizontal tabs in the same color as large plates.
|-
|[[File:1946 Wisconsin license plate.jpeg|150px]]<br>[[File:WI 46 with 47 tab.jpg|150x150px]]<br>[[File:Blank License Plate Shape.svg|150px]]<br>[[File:WI 1947 w 1949 tab.jpg|150x150px]]<br>[[File:Blank License Plate Shape.svg|150px]]
|1946–49
